y B8; Play W3; Play W1\n\nPlay F3 is bad, Play B5 is bad, Play B4 is not great, etc.\n\nPlay W9 seems reasonable.\n\nPlay T6 also.\n\nBut T6 could be useful for 456.\n\nSince previous player played T4, T4 is available, so T6 might be good to keep.\n\nPlayer played T4, so T4 is out, but unshown T4:3, so others might have it.\n\nFor me, if I keep T6, I can aim for T4,T5,T6 chow.\n\nSimilarly, B5 for B4,B5,B6.\n\nSo both T6 and B5 are useful.\n\nB5 is more useful because I have B4.\n\nT6 I have only one, so less critical.\n\nSo Play T6 might be better than Play B5.\n\nPlay B5 is discarding B5, which is bad.\n\nPlay T6 is discarding T6.\n\nSo Play T6 is an option.\n\nSimilarly, Play J2.\n\nBut let's decide.\n\nI think the best discards are Play W9, Play B8, or Play T6.\n\nAmong them, Play W9 might be best because it's a terminal and not connected.\n\nAlso, in the hand, W9 is the only high Character, while I have low and middle.\n\nSimilarly, for Dots, I have low B2, middle B4,B5, so B8 is high, but I have B2, so discarding B8 might be good, but B2 is also low.\n\nBut B2 can connect to B4 with B3.\n\nW9 has no connection to W1,W3.\n\nW1 and W3 can connect with W2, W9 needs W7,W8.\n\nSo W9 is more isolated.\n\nTherefore, Play W9 is a good choice.\n\nPlay B8 is similar, but for Dots, I have more tiles, so B8 might be less isolated relatively.\n\nBut still.\n\nI think Play W9.\n\nMoreover, in the patterns, if I keep W1 and W3, I can go for Pure Straight or Mixed Straight later.\n\nBut for now, discard W9.\n\nSo I'll go with Play W9.", "output": "Situation: The prevailing wind is East, and my seat wind is West. My hand contains a mix of suited and honor tiles, with multiple Dots (B) tiles (B2, B4x2, B5, B8), some Characters (W1, W3, W9), one Bamboo (T6), and honors (F3x2, F4x2, J2). I have no exposed melds, and the game is in the early stages with minimal history\u2014only the across player discarded B5 and the previous player discarded T4. The unshown tiles indicate most tiles are plentiful, except T4 and B5 have three copies unshown (one discarded each). My hand is flexible but has redundant or isolated elements: W9 is a high terminal not connected to my other Character tiles (W1 and W3, which could form part of a 123 chow with W2), while B8 is a high Dots tile with no immediate connection to my middle Dots (B4/B5). Keeping W9 limits my ability to focus on a cohesive hand, as it conflicts with potential low-end sequences in Characters.\n\nEvaluation: Key promising discards include:\n- **Play W9**: Discarding this isolated terminal frees me to focus on building a 123 chow in Characters with W1/W3 (needing W2) or other Dots-based sequences. W9 has low utility since it requires W7/W8 for a 789 chow, but my existing W1/W3 suggest a low-end strategy is more viable. This discard supports patterns like Mixed Straight (8 points) or Pure Straight (16 points) by reducing dead wood.\n- **Play B8**: Similar to W9, B8 is a high terminal that doesn't connect to my Dots (B2, B4, B5). Discarding it streamlines the hand for middle sequences like B3-B4-B5 or B4-B5-B6, aiding patterns such as Pure Triple Chow (24 points) or Outside Hand (2 points). However, B8 has slightly better integration than W9 due to more Dots tiles in hand.\n- **Play T6**: Discarding the lone Bamboo tile removes a single-suit liability. T6 could form part of a 456 chow (with T4/T5), but with only one Bamboo tile, this is unlikely. Keeping it risks cluttering the hand for suit-focused patterns. This discard is safe but less impactful than removing a terminal.\n- **Avoid Play B5/Play B4/Play F3**: Discarding B5 breaks the critical link for B4-B5 chows (e.g., with B3/B6), while discarding one B4 reduces pung potential. Discarding F3 (seat wind) sacrifices a high-value pattern (Seat Wind, 2 points), which is suboptimal.\n\nReason: Play W9 is the strongest move because W9 is the most isolated tile\u2014unconnected to my other Character tiles and conflicting with a low-end focus. Discarding it prioritizes hand cohesion: W1 and W3 can efficiently form a 123 chow (with W2), supporting higher-value patterns like Mixed Straight (8 points) or Pure Straight (16 points). In contrast, Play B8 is viable but less urgent, as B8 has marginal Dots integration (B2 could anchor a low end), and Play T6 discards a tile with marginal chow potential. Early-game strategy favors shedding terminals that don't align with existing sequences, and W9 fits this perfectly. Additionally, unshown W9 tiles are abundant (4 copies), reducing discard risk.\n\nAnswer: Play W9"}
